Face diagonals are the diagonals that lie on a face of the cube. Since each face is a square, the diagonal of a face can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem. For a square with side length 1, the diagonal would be √(1² + 1²) = √2. So, each face diagonal has length √2.

Then there are the space diagonals, which are the diagonals that go through the interior of the cube from one vertex to the opposite vertex. These are not on any face, but connect two vertices that aren't adjacent. For a cube with side length 1, the space diagonal can be calculated using the 3D version of the Pythagorean theorem. 

